# XCU logger
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Where the messages go is implementation-defined, so a script cannot read
# them back; the tests judge the exit status, the silence of the standard
# streams and the handling of input sources. The level emerg is not
# exercised because it may be forwarded to every user's terminal. Messages
# carry the tag posixcheck.

assertion  XCU.logger.001
anchor     utilities/logger.html#OPERANDS
anchor     utilities/logger.html#EXIT STATUS
anchor     utilities/logger.html#STDOUT
class      required
says       logger with string operands (and a tag) exits with status zero and
           writes nothing to standard output or standard error.
test       t/001-string-operands.sh

assertion  XCU.logger.002
anchor     utilities/logger.html#OPTIONS
class      required
says       -p accepts the facilities user and local0 to local7 and the levels
           alert, crit, err, warning, notice, info and debug.
test       t/002-priorities.sh

assertion  XCU.logger.003
anchor     utilities/logger.html#STDIN
anchor     utilities/logger.html#EXIT STATUS
class      required
says       Without string operands and without -f, logger reads message lines
           from standard input and exits with status zero, writing nothing to
           standard output.
test       t/003-standard-input.sh

assertion  XCU.logger.004
anchor     utilities/logger.html#OPTIONS
anchor     utilities/logger.html#EXIT STATUS
class      required
says       -f FILE reads the message lines from FILE, with status zero, and a
           FILE that cannot be read gives a status above zero.
test       t/004-file-option.sh

assertion  XCU.logger.005
anchor     utilities/logger.html#OPTIONS
class      required
says       -i and -t TAG together with a string operand are accepted: status
           zero and nothing written to standard output or standard error.
test       t/005-pid-and-tag.sh

assertion  XCU.logger.006
anchor     utilities/logger.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     utilities/logger.html#OPTIONS
class      untestable
says       The messages are sent to the logging facility with a header holding
           at least a timestamp and the tag, at the given facility and level
           (user.notice by default), and each non-empty input line is a
           separate message.
reason     The logging facility and the place the messages end up are
           implementation-defined, so a script cannot find what was logged.
